    "description": "Iranian university sport connects student competition with international university-sport institutions and educational activities. Its documented scope includes university teams, national competitions, volunteering, and preparation for international events. [s1][s2][s3]",
    "tags": ["University sport", "Student athletes", "Futsal", "FISU", "Sports volunteering"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T07:28:05Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T07:28:05Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "University Sport in Iran: Competition, Students and International Links",
      "description": "Explore Iran’s university-sport federation, a documented student futsal competition, and the educational and volunteer activities surrounding university sport."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "international-framework",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A university-based sporting framework",
        "content": "Iranian university sport has an established international connection through the National University Sports Federation of Iran. FISU, the International University Sports Federation, records Iran’s membership from 1967. Its member directory also identifies Tehran as the host of the World University Wrestling Championship in 1996. These are distinct milestones: affiliation established an institutional relationship, while hosting brought an international student competition to Iran. [s1]\n\nFor an international reader, the central distinction is between a university-sport framework and the wider professional club system. A university name in a competition identifies an institutional affiliation; it does not by itself explain an athlete’s professional experience, scholarship arrangements, or eligibility for another event. Those questions require the regulations of the particular competition. The verified examples here establish participation and institutional connections, rather than a universal rule for every Iranian student athlete. [s1][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "futsal-example",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A national competition with an international connection",
        "content": "A concrete example comes from futsal, the indoor form of association football. In a report dated 2 May 2026, the Asian University Sports Federation identified Payame Noor University of Qom as the winner of Iran’s first National Student Futsal League. It reported that the team defeated Shahid Beheshti University in the final. The final stage took place at Tarbiat Modares University, with participating universities also including Tehran, Isfahan, and Zabol. [s3]\n\nThe report connected the title to qualification for the 2026 FISU World University Championship Futsal in Poland. That statement documents a reported qualification outcome; it does not establish that the team subsequently travelled, competed, or achieved a particular international result. Keeping these stages separate matters when interpreting university-sport news. A domestic title, an international entry, and a completed international appearance are different achievements. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "education-and-volunteering",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Participation beyond the playing field",
        "content": "University sport also provides roles beyond competing. FISU reported in July 2020 that Iran’s university-sport federation and the Sport Sciences Research Institute of Iran organised educational workshops introducing students to FISU. Their subjects included international sports events, educational programmes, scholarships, and the organisation’s structure. [s2]\n\nThe same account described student volunteer associations and webinars about volunteer recruitment, motivations, obstacles, and management. This is evidence of organised educational activity at that time, rather than proof that the same associations remain active or retain their original membership. It nevertheless shows why university sport should be understood as more than a medal table: students can encounter event organisation and international sports institutions through educational and volunteer work. [s2]\n\nTaken together, the affiliation record, futsal example, and educational programme illustrate different parts of the field. They do not measure how evenly opportunities are distributed between campuses or how many students participate regularly. Competition reports make achievements visible; evaluating everyday access requires separate evidence about facilities, schedules, costs, and participation. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
