    "description": "Community sport concerns opportunities for everyday physical activity beyond elite competition. In Iran, national surveys and local research help explain participation barriers, but physical-activity measurements should not be mistaken for counts of community-sport members. [s1][s2]",
    "tags": ["Community sport", "Physical activity", "Active transport", "Public space", "Participation", "Iran"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T07:28:05Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T07:28:05Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Community Sport in Iran: Everyday Activity and Access",
      "description": "Understand everyday physical activity in Iran, the limits of national survey evidence, and barriers involving time, health, neighbourhoods, and access."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "scope",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Understanding everyday participation",
        "content": "Community sport shifts attention from winning competitions to the conditions that allow people to be active in ordinary life. The World Health Organization defines physical activity broadly: movement can occur during recreation, transport, work, or domestic tasks. Walking, cycling, wheeling, play, and sport can all contribute. This provides a useful framework for examining Iran without treating formal club membership as the only meaningful form of participation. [s3]\n\nThe branches in this guide distinguish walking, cycling, mountaineering, running, park exercise, and physical activity for older people. They raise different questions about access. A journey on foot involves destinations and streets; recreational exercise involves opportunities to spend leisure time actively. WHO’s distinction between transport and leisure activity helps explain why a single participation measure cannot describe every branch equally well. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "national-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What national surveys can establish",
        "content": "An analysis of Iran’s STEPS 2021 survey examined physical activity across work, transport, and recreation. STEPS is a framework for monitoring risk factors for noncommunicable diseases. Its physical-activity findings therefore concern a broader subject than organised community sport: a respondent may accumulate activity through work or travel without attending an exercise session. Conversely, participation in a recreational activity does not automatically establish sufficient total activity. [s1]\n\nThe study found substantial insufficient activity and differences by sex, age, and residential setting. These findings make access an important subject, but they do not identify the performance of a particular municipality or programme. The observations were collected during the COVID-19 pandemic, and the research was observational. They should not be presented as a direct measurement of conditions at the editorial cutoff or as proof that one factor caused inactivity. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "barriers",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Time, health, and competing responsibilities",
        "content": "A separate analysis of STEPS 2021 responses investigated barriers to physical activity. Work commitments, family responsibilities, and physical conditions such as illness, pain, injury, disability, and fatigue were prominent reported obstacles. The findings challenge the assumption that inactivity can be explained simply by lack of interest. People may value activity while finding that their schedules or physical circumstances make participation difficult. [s2]\n\nThe researchers grouped barriers into personal, interpersonal, economic, cultural, and environmental domains. These are analytical categories, not mutually exclusive descriptions of individuals. A person can encounter several obstacles at once. The study also examined associations between barrier domains and activity, but its cross-sectional design cannot establish that removing a reported obstacle would produce a particular increase in participation. [s2]\n\nA reasonable editorial implication is that the existence of a facility is only one part of access. Whether an opportunity fits people’s available time and circumstances also deserves examination. That is an interpretation of the barrier evidence, rather than a verified evaluation of a specific Iranian programme. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "local-context",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why neighbourhood evidence matters",
        "content": "Research with Tehran residents identified personal, social, and environmental barriers to active transport, including infrastructure problems. This local qualitative evidence helps explain experiences that a national prevalence figure cannot capture, but it does not measure how common each problem is across Iran. [s4]\n\nThe distinction is especially important for older participants. A study of recently retired people in Tehran Province associated leisure-walking frequency with social support, nearby green spaces, roads, intersections, and other neighbourhood characteristics. Its models also identified differences between men and women. These findings support examining the relationship between people and their surroundings, while remaining specific to the population studied. They cannot establish the circumstances of all older Iranians or every rural community. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s4", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-the-field",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading participation claims carefully",
        "content": "The strongest account of community sport combines clearly defined activity measures with evidence about everyday opportunities. National surveys supply a population perspective; local studies help explain how activity fits into particular settings. Neither should silently stand in for the other. For readers exploring this guide, the useful questions are what activity is being measured, whose experience is represented, and whether the evidence concerns a completed programme, a reported obstacle, or a broader interpretation. [s1][s2][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s4"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
