    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Walking connects everyday travel with recreational physical activity in Iran. Research from Tehran shows how destinations, neighbourhood conditions, and social circumstances shape walking, while leaving important gaps in nationally comparable evidence. [s1][s2]",
    "tags": ["Walking", "Walkability", "Active transport", "Tehran", "Older adults", "Public space"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T07:28:05Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T07:28:05Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Walking in Iran: Everyday Journeys, Recreation and Walkability",
      "description": "Explore walking in Iran through research on everyday travel, neighbourhood conditions, retirement, and the limits of available participation evidence."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "purposes",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "One activity, different purposes",
        "content": "Walking can be a journey, a recreational activity, or part of a longer trip using public transport. Research with Tehran residents describes walking for shopping, short journeys, exercise, and recreation. These purposes matter because they place different demands on a neighbourhood: reaching a destination and enjoying a leisure walk are related experiences, but they are not identical. [s1]\n\nWHO includes movement for transport and leisure within physical activity. That definition helps explain walking’s place in community sport without requiring it to be an organised competition. It also avoids treating every pedestrian journey as a deliberately planned workout. The activity can be woven into ordinary life, even when the person making the journey does not identify as a sports participant. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "street-experience",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Walking depends on the journey",
        "content": "A qualitative study conducted in Tehran during 2024 examined perceptions of walking and cycling. Participants described walking as convenient for short trips and discussed combining it with public transport. They also identified barriers involving infrastructure, travel distance, and air pollution. These are reported experiences and perceptions, not measurements of the medical risk of a particular route or evidence that every neighbourhood has the same conditions. [s1]\n\nThe study’s value lies in explaining how decisions arise in everyday circumstances. Its findings do not provide a national walking rate or rank Iranian cities by walkability. Nor should findings specific to cycling automatically be applied to pedestrians: the same research discusses both modes, but their equipment, infrastructure, and social contexts differ. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "retirement-and-neighbourhood",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Retirement, companionship, and nearby destinations",
        "content": "Research on recently retired adults in Tehran Province examined leisure-walking frequency alongside personal and neighbourhood characteristics. The published abstract identifies associations involving social support, marital status, vehicle ownership, green spaces, slopes or steps, and major roads and intersections. This places walking within a social and spatial setting rather than explaining it solely as an individual preference. [s2]\n\nThe models also identified differences between men and women. Being divorced or widowed was particularly influential in the model of low walking levels among men, while proximity to shopping areas was especially important for women. These are findings for the sampled retirees and the models used; they are not fixed characteristics of Iranian men and women generally. The evidence suggests questions about companionship and useful nearby destinations, rather than a single design solution for every resident. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "interpretation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What the evidence supports",
        "content": "Walking is best understood here as an everyday activity whose opportunities depend partly on surroundings and circumstances. The inspected Iranian studies explain particular experiences and associations; they do not establish a continuous national trend. WHO’s broader guidance recognises that physical activity contributes to health and that activity of different kinds counts. This page therefore offers context for understanding walking, without prescribing a personal target or presenting local findings as a nationwide assessment. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
